<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eli Whitney</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1765">1765</a>–<a href="/years/1825">1825</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eli Whitney was an American inventor who patented the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton-gin">cotton gin</a> in 1794, a machine that separated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> fibres from their seeds far faster than by hand. It made short-staple cotton profitable across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-south">American South</a> and fuelled the expansion of cotton plantations and of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>; he also promoted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/interchangeable-parts">interchangeable parts</a> in the manufacture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eli-whitney.jpg" alt="Eli Whitney" width="320" height="413" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Samuel Finley Breese Morse · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Eli_Whitney_by_Samuel_Finley_Breese_Morse_1822.jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Eli Whitney was born in Westborough, Massachusetts, on 8 December 1765, the eldest child of the prosperous farmer Eli Whitney Sr. and Elizabeth Fay, who died when he was 11. His stepmother opposed his wish to attend college, so he worked as a farm labourer and schoolteacher to save money, prepared at Leicester Academy, and entered Yale in 1789.</p><p>He graduated Phi Beta Kappa in 1792 and, short of funds, accepted a post as a private tutor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>. Instead he was persuaded to visit <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/georgia">Georgia</a>, where Catherine Littlefield Greene, widow of the Revolutionary hero Nathanael Greene, invited him to her plantation, Mulberry Grove. Her manager, Phineas Miller, became his business partner.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>At Mulberry Grove businessmen discussed the need for a machine to separate short-staple upland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> from its seeds, work then done by hand at about a pound of lint a day. In a few weeks Whitney made a model: a wooden drum set with hooks that pulled the fibres through a mesh too fine for the seeds.</p><p>A single gin could clean up to 55 pounds a day. He applied for a patent on 28 October 1793 and received it on 14 March 1794. Resentment, the machine's simplicity and the primitive state of patent law led to infringement, and lawsuits consumed their profits. Their company ended in 1797. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">Cotton</a> exports rose from under 500,000 pounds in 1793 to 93 million pounds by 1810.</p><p>By 1798 Whitney was nearly bankrupt, and in January he won a government contract to deliver between 10,000 and 15,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/musket">muskets</a>, though he had never made a gun. He first spoke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/interchangeable-parts">interchangeable parts</a> ten months later. The arms were delivered only in 1809. Historian Merritt Roe Smith concludes that his 1801 demonstration of interchangeability was staged. The idea long predated him, and others achieved it in their armories first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Whitney used his Yale connections, among them the Treasury Secretary Oliver Wolcott Jr., to build his arms business, and in 1817 he married Henrietta Edwards, daughter of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/connecticut">Connecticut</a> political leader Pierpont Edwards. He died of prostate cancer in New Haven on 8 January 1825. His son, Eli Whitney III, took over the armory. Whitney's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton-gin">cotton gin</a> made upland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> profitable and strengthened the economic foundation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>. Cotton became the chief export of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, over half its value from 1820 to 1860.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eli Whitney” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…shipping and, from the 1790s, textile mills in New England dominated, while in the South plantations worked by enslaved people of African descent produced tobacco, rice and above all cotton, whose output soared after <b>Eli Whitney</b>'s cotton gin of 1793; by 1860 nearly four million people were enslaved.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Westborough, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. Died in New Haven, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-states">the United States</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=42.27,-71.62&amp;year=1804" aria-label="Westborough on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2812 660 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Eli Whitney"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3262 930h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3262 930h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=42.27,-71.62&amp;year=1804">What was here in 1804? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
